sec.p:
Within-population diversity, πw, was estimated as
(1)
πw=piqi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \pi_{w}=p_{i} q_{i} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where pi and qi refer to the frequencies of alleles p and q within a single population, population i. dXY, between-population diversity, was estimated using
(2)
dXY=pi(1−pj)+pj(1−pi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d_{X Y}=p_{i}\left(1-p_{j}\right)+p_{j}\left(1-p_{i}\right) \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where i and j refer to a pair of populations, i and j. We also calculate πw\begin{document}$\pi_{w}$\end{document} (mean πw between i and j). FST, relative divergence, is estimated as
(3)
FST=dxy−πwdxy \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} F_{S T}=\frac{d_{x y}-\pi_{w}}{d_{x y}} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Nei’s standard genetic distance was calculated as
(4)
D=dXY−πw― \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} D=d_{X Y}-\bar{\pi}_{w} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
